After /cd, the new directory's [permissions] deny patterns now block bash commands for the rest of the session. Committed as d8a187f7; build, test, clippy and fmt all pass.

What was wrong: the deny list was copied into the bash tool once, when the tool was built. Nothing read later, including anything /cd found, could reach it. The null clause didn't apply.

What changed:
• **`src/cd_deny.rs` (new):** one session-wide deny list that only ever grows, read at the moment a command runs. Each bash tool also gets a handle to it as a field, and tests pass their own local list so they never touch the shared one.
• **Both bash runners check it.** My grep found only two places that apply the user's deny list: the main agent's bash (`StreamingBashTool::execute`) and the sub-agent bash (`UserDenyBashTool). Both now check the startup list first, then whatever /cd` has added. The refusal wording is unchanged.
• **`/cd in src/dispatch.rs:** reads the new directory's .yoyo.toml once, using the same parser startup uses (parse_permissions_from_config`). It adds the deny entries and prints the note from that same read. There's no trust check, because a deny entry can only block things, never allow them.
• **The /cd note (src/cd_config_note.rs):** it now says the deny patterns are enforced on bash, that another `/cd` won't remove them, and that file tools never read this list. It also says allow, [directories], hooks and MCP servers are still not reloaded. The permissions entry now reads [permissions] allow, because only that half is still ignored. A config with only an allow list prints the same text as before. I updated the existing tests to the new wording and deleted none.

Tests:
• The main agent's bash refuses an entry added after it was built, and an unrelated command still runs. The sub-agent bash refuses the same entry.
• Merging lists never drops an entry and removes duplicates, and a second /cd adds without removing.
• New exact-text checks on the note, plus one confirming it still prints at warning level when the file only sets deny.
• Positive control: I disabled the new check in the main agent's bash and parent_bash_refuses_an_entry_added_after_build failed. I restored it in the same command and the test passed again.

One fix along the way: the project's lock-recovery test rejected my hand-written lock handling, so I switched it to the shared sync_util::lock_or_recover helper.

Still not covered:
• `/cd still doesn't reload allow, [directories]`, hooks, MCP servers or skills.
• These deny entries apply to bash only. The file tools have never read permissions.deny, at startup or after /cd.
• /run, ! commands and background jobs never checked the user deny list before this change, and still don't.
• Entries are never removed during a session.

I wrote this up in a new ARCHITECTURE.md entry and a short "After /cd" section in docs/src/configuration/permissions.md. I also commented on #869 with what landed and what remains, and left the issue open.
